  "body": "Found by the review of PR 1464 (the kernel-side `.sc` reader, part of issue 1456).\n\nWhat is missing:\n- NaN or huge coordinates in a `.sc` body are not refused in the Solidcraft reader itself; it relies on the shared STEP-side checks, and no test feeds such a body through `import_solidcraft`.\n- The fuzz-style test `damaged_files_never_panic_or_hang` damages bytes of the container, so almost every copy stops at the CRC-32 check and the body decoder (where a hang or an oversized allocation could live) is barely reached.\n\nWanted:\n- A test that builds a `.sc` with a valid container and checksum around a body carrying NaN, infinite and out-of-range coordinates, and asserts a named refusal.\n- A fuzz-style test that damages the decompressed body and re-wraps it with a correct CRC, so the decoder itself is exercised; with a time bound so a hang fails the test.\n\nThis is an importer of untrusted files (Prime Directive: corrupt models give a clean error, never a crash or hang). It must land before the reader is wired into the app or MCP.\n\nDone means: both tests exist in `crates/kernel/tests/` and pass; any defect they show is fixed in the reader with a named error.\n",
